WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.472 --> 00:00:13.595
And today's feature presenter
is Professor Banny Banerjee.

00:00:14.645 --> 00:00:18.315
Banny Banerjee is Professor of the
Practice at the Stanford School of Earth,

00:00:18.315 --> 00:00:20.695
Energy, and Environmental Sciences.

00:00:20.695 --> 00:00:24.165
He is also the co-founder and
director of Stanford Change Labs, and

00:00:24.165 --> 00:00:27.890
teaches design innovation and
strategy at the Stanford D School.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:32.030
Before his academic appointment at
Stanford, Banny spent nearly a decade as

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:36.400
a technology innovation lead at
the renowned design firm IDEO.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.080
His area of expertise is to use design
thinking for strategic initiative and

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.310
large scale transformations directed
towards sustainable futures.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.880
Regarded as one of the leading experts
in innovation strategy in the area

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.000
of sustainable transformations,
he's now working with the Dean

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.560
of the Stanford School of Earth, Energy
and Environmental Sciences, Pamela Matson,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.420
to shape a new academic program
around systems transformation.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.200
And now I'd like to turn
the floor over to Banny.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.610
&gt;&gt; Good day everyone.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.170
Very nice to meet you
albeit in digital space,

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.030
hope to meet you some day in person.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:14.630
What I would like to speak about to day is
essentially the types of innovations that

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:19.590
you need when the challenges
have a certain

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:24.310
set of attributes and particularly
those that involve complexity in scale.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:29.950
What I'm going to cover
today are a set of topics.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.650
I'm going to break it down in this way.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:36.250
First and
foremost I would like to make a case for

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.250
a certain style of innovation
that we call systems innovation.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:44.290
But since we are going into
certain nuances of innovation, and

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:47.260
the word is used in many ways,

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:52.490
I would like to spend some time on
innovation itself and define innovation.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:56.310
And then go into two different
types of methodologies,

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:00.250
one of them is called design thinking,
which many of you might be familiar with.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.470
And another which we
call system acupuncture.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:10.320
At that point in time, you might ask when
do you use what type of methodology,

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.960
so we'll go a little bit into
different types of challenges and

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:20.030
when to approach,
how to pick different methodologies.

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:25.400
But in doing that,
we will have touched upon issues of scale,

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.250
and some other attributes about scale.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.260
But this is also a topic that is very,

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:36.672
very relevant in terms of what types
of leadership we need for the future.

00:02:36.672 --> 00:02:42.220
So we'll spend a little time on this
notion of transformative leadership.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:44.098
So we dive in.

00:02:44.098 --> 00:02:48.719
What I'd like to do before I get
into the topic of innovation is,

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:54.212
take a few steps back and talk about
the world we find ourselves in right now.

00:02:54.212 --> 00:02:58.231
So if we look at
the mega-trends in the world,

00:02:58.231 --> 00:03:01.951
a few very, very failing things jump out.

00:03:01.951 --> 00:03:07.139
One of the things that we found
from a previous few decades

00:03:07.139 --> 00:03:14.598
is that our global economies seem to be
increasingly fragile with very stochastic

00:03:14.598 --> 00:03:20.783
behavior that are caused by
perturbations anywhere on the planet.

00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:28.157
And so, we need to prepare for economic
downturn, upheavals in rapid time frames.

00:03:28.157 --> 00:03:34.426
And our long-term futures in terms
of whether it business futures,

00:03:34.426 --> 00:03:41.670
or in terms of larger issues such
as civilization or societal issues.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.880
Being questioned by
increasing turbulence and

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.200
large scale challenges that essentially
question the very basis of stability.

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:53.490
And, of course, as we all realize,

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:58.530
our globe now is much more
connected than it ever was.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:01.650
But it is not an equitable system and

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:06.595
the sharp inequities cause problems unto
itself as we are seeing in the larger

00:04:06.595 --> 00:04:12.365
political issues that
are prevalent in the planet today.

00:04:12.365 --> 00:04:17.645
And these are no longer abstract with
political instability and geopolitical

00:04:17.645 --> 00:04:23.635
shifts that changes the policy landscape,
or issues of security, etc.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:30.730
In terms of businesses, we are seeing
rapidly shifting markets, very

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:36.656
new players, and technology regimes that
are changing at an increasing clock tick.

00:04:36.656 --> 00:04:41.470
But there's something
else that's going on.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:48.010
Given that we are in the midst
of such big changes, and it

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:52.830
is not a secret in the general population
that we are in the midst of changes.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.210
The sensibilities are changing and

00:04:55.210 --> 00:05:01.530
who we are catering to with what kind
of definitions of success is changing.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.906
And that has a lot to do with
how we craft our position.

00:05:04.906 --> 00:05:10.024
And how our brand is perceived and
how our value systems are framed.

00:05:10.024 --> 00:05:17.044
And let's not forget that there are very
large changes taking place such as,

00:05:17.044 --> 00:05:21.904
climate change,
insecurity in our questions around

00:05:21.904 --> 00:05:26.656
continued security around food,
water, energy,

00:05:26.656 --> 00:05:32.090
as we add more than a billion
people in the next few decades.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:37.817
And then let's also not forget, that we
normally think about human issues and

00:05:37.817 --> 00:05:40.783
animal-centric kind of point of view.

00:05:40.783 --> 00:05:45.228
But we're in the midst of an environmental
crisis of the sort we haven't seen since

00:05:45.228 --> 00:05:46.826
the dinosaurs went extinct.

00:05:46.826 --> 00:05:51.538
And a whole lot of our largest systems
actually rely on the fact that we have

00:05:51.538 --> 00:05:52.906
a stable biosphere.

00:05:52.906 --> 00:05:56.703
So that's something that's
already happening and

00:05:56.703 --> 00:06:01.617
we will be getting to 9 billion
people on a limited size of a planet,

00:06:01.617 --> 00:06:06.906
which has already had more resources
drawn from it than it can recharge.

00:06:06.906 --> 00:06:10.640
So these are all very very large trends,
and one would say that, okay,

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.766
how does it affect my day or my business?

00:06:12.766 --> 00:06:17.270
But I would like to argue that
without keeping this in mind,

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:23.759
one might be creating not only overlooking
an opportunity to create a better future,

00:06:23.759 --> 00:06:29.369
but also building in risk into our
businesses today or our agendas today.

00:06:29.369 --> 00:06:35.849
So, what I would like to claim is that all
these mega-trends indicate a world order,

00:06:35.849 --> 00:06:42.910
which is very different from the one in
which our prevalent thinking was shaped.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:44.928
So if we are to meet the future,

00:06:44.928 --> 00:06:50.670
we should essentially evaluate what
is in store for us in the future and

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:55.880
what kind of challenges we are facing,
and perhaps retool our ways of thinking.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:07:00.506
And some of these challenges
are very large in scale.

00:07:00.506 --> 00:07:05.180
And we are often motivated by them,
or we are in the midst of directly or

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:07.350
indirectly working on them.

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:09.070
And the question is,

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:14.180
to what extent can we move the needles on
any of the challenges that we are facing?

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:19.500
And also recognizing that not
all problems are linear and

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.310
a lot of problems
are exponential in scale.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.029
But meanwhile what we are very,

00:07:24.029 --> 00:07:27.666
very good at doing are things such
as the line that the S scale shows.

00:07:27.666 --> 00:07:29.284
And that's when the going is good.

00:07:29.284 --> 00:07:35.711
Where we are able to it for a period,
then it scales up and then it levels off.

00:07:35.711 --> 00:07:40.442
And that's when we are successful,
but that creates a theoretical

00:07:40.442 --> 00:07:44.427
gap if the problem is increasing
at an exponential rate.

00:07:44.427 --> 00:07:47.955
And that essentially begs the question,

00:07:47.955 --> 00:07:53.370
how can we meet nonlinear problems
with nonlinear solutions?

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:59.040
And then the issue of time,
this is the symbol for the time constant.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.897
And every system out there
Has a native time constant,

00:08:03.897 --> 00:08:08.550
whether it's a business system or
an institutional system or

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:14.378
a cultural system or the cells in our
body or some geological force out there.

00:08:14.378 --> 00:08:21.400
It goes from seconds to years to eons,
but this has a clock tick.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:25.982
And it turns out that the clock tick of
change that we are seeing is faster than

00:08:25.982 --> 00:08:28.774
the clock tick of many
of our organizations and

00:08:28.774 --> 00:08:30.900
many of our institutions.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.460
And so
we need to find ways of intelligently

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:34.670
dealing with the issue of time.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:40.568
So what we're looking at when
we take a few steps back is

00:08:40.568 --> 00:08:45.628
that increasingly as leaders,
as people who are managing

00:08:45.628 --> 00:08:49.672
day-to-day operations as well as big ones.

00:08:49.672 --> 00:08:54.005
We are looking at challenges that
are no longer to be addressed by

00:08:54.005 --> 00:08:58.740
businesses' usual methods,
because these challenges are scaled,

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:04.280
they're complex, often with time
frames that seem unrealistic.

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:07.050
And so we need new ways of thinking.

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:13.250
So what we need is a kind of innovation,

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:17.310
and I'll get into the definition
of innovation in just a bit,

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:20.860
but of the type of innovation that
we haven't really seen before.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:24.810
So we need to, in a sense,
innovate on innovation.

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:29.927
And what I think we need is a very
radical form of innovation that takes

00:09:29.927 --> 00:09:34.882
on the complexity and the scale and
the urgency of the challenges.

00:09:34.882 --> 00:09:39.651
So I've been using the word innovation,
it gets used in very many different ways.

00:09:39.651 --> 00:09:44.294
People talk of technology as innovation,
and often innovations and

00:09:44.294 --> 00:09:49.989
innovation get used synonymously, and
a clever technology is called innovation.

00:09:49.989 --> 00:09:55.965
The way I mean it it is essentially a way
of demarcating something that's innovative

00:09:55.965 --> 00:10:01.619
and something that's not, by saying,
does it outperform business as usual?

00:10:01.619 --> 00:10:07.225
Does it outperform the normative modes of
working in order to drive completely new

00:10:07.225 --> 00:10:13.013
outcomes, completely new behaviors,
new paradigms that rewrite the rule books?

00:10:13.013 --> 00:10:15.749
Can we change the behavior of the system?

00:10:15.749 --> 00:10:19.360
And can we send it in a different
direction in the future?

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:25.290
And it's a broader frame for innovation.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:29.920
So everything I'm going to talk about
about innovation is with this frame.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:34.210
Which is, we already have very,
very good processes and

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:37.260
we've attained a certain kind
of operational excellence.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:41.220
But it turns out that operational
excellence is not good enough anymore.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:44.680
So how can we outperform
the business as usual?

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:47.660
Okay, so that's the thing.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:52.790
But I would like to say that this is
not just a matter of new processes and

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:58.040
skills, but it also involves new
mindsets and new leadership models.

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:02.900
And the leadership models would lead
to new organizational cultures and

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:08.170
ecosystems that would allow
these processes to take root so

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:10.650
that you can outperform
the business decision.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:16.461
Now mindsets,
there are many dimensions of mindsets.

00:11:16.461 --> 00:11:20.284
Today, I'm going to touch
on a few mindsets, and

00:11:20.284 --> 00:11:26.510
I'm going to frame a few very key failure
modes that you need to watch out for.

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:30.937
Because if you're falling into that trap,
it means that you've written away

00:11:30.937 --> 00:11:34.760
the opportunities that you get if
you are doing systems thinking and

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:37.056
if you are doing innovation correctly.

00:11:37.056 --> 00:11:42.307
So first and foremost,
I would say that there is such a thing

00:11:42.307 --> 00:11:48.504
as innovation-blindness,
which means that your theory of change or

00:11:48.504 --> 00:11:55.250
success might not be directed towards
outperforming the normative modes.

00:11:55.250 --> 00:12:00.910
You have placed too big
a faith in the current system.

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:05.590
And you're drawing your current theory
of success based on what you've done

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:08.680
in the past, rather than what
you ought to do in the future.

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:13.870
And you're essentially writing away
the disproportionate wins you could have

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:16.040
if you were to bring in
an innovation agenda.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:21.783
Now, what I'm going to do, in having
kind of framed innovation this way,

00:12:21.783 --> 00:12:25.087
is that I'm going to talk about
two different techniques.

00:12:25.087 --> 00:12:27.055
And I'm going to talk
about design thinking and

00:12:27.055 --> 00:12:29.085
then go into systems thinking.

00:12:29.085 --> 00:12:34.135
Design thinking has become quite popular,
a lot of you know about this.

00:12:34.135 --> 00:12:38.813
But I'll give you a little bit of
history and also perhaps a case

00:12:38.813 --> 00:12:43.764
that'll help to explain how I am
thinking about design thinking.

00:12:43.764 --> 00:12:48.732
Now design thinking came out
of the design field roughly

00:12:48.732 --> 00:12:53.499
around the late 1990s at
the turn of the century.

00:12:53.499 --> 00:12:58.251
Where designers realized that
what they had been asked to do

00:12:58.251 --> 00:13:02.255
was not just operational products and
services.

00:13:02.255 --> 00:13:07.145
But they were also being asked to
solve questions that were somewhat

00:13:07.145 --> 00:13:12.920
ill-defined and had to do with things that
had to be conceived of in the future.

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:17.251
And it turned out that the way designers
think is quite different from how

00:13:17.251 --> 00:13:18.963
different fields operate.

00:13:18.963 --> 00:13:22.659
So just as engineering is very
different from political science,

00:13:22.659 --> 00:13:27.015
it's very different from economics,
design is also quite different, and so

00:13:27.015 --> 00:13:30.020
this notion of design thinking came about.

00:13:30.020 --> 00:13:34.540
And it started getting used in very many
different ways to create experiences of

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:39.740
the future to which one could
design products and services.

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:43.860
All designing systems
are by the largest system,

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:47.532
that did many things with many
components or strategies.

00:13:47.532 --> 00:13:51.431
Strategies as opposed to tactics,
which mean that plans for

00:13:51.431 --> 00:13:55.430
longer-term futures as opposed
to shorter-term futures.

00:13:55.430 --> 00:14:01.030
And interventions to challenges that
didn't seem to be on any one person's

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:06.460
agenda, but they needed to be
done in a multi-stakeholder way.

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:11.180
But also very importantly, creating
visions of the future to shoot for.

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:15.800
So in a sense, you need a certain
imagination to shoot for, for

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:17.870
a certain future that you want, so

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:21.530
that you can direct your plans and
your actions towards them.

00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:24.110
And design thinking started
getting used for that.

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:27.460
So let me take you to a very simple case.

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:32.117
This is a student project that was
done at Stanford in the D School.

00:14:32.117 --> 00:14:35.760
And it's a very hands-on class.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:38.613
And students from all walks of life and

00:14:38.613 --> 00:14:44.425
very different disciplines get brought
in into introductory design class.

00:14:44.425 --> 00:14:48.507
And this is a journey of
a set of students, a team,

00:14:48.507 --> 00:14:51.336
in an introductory design class.

00:14:51.336 --> 00:14:56.308
So they were given a challenge
around infant mortality.

00:14:56.308 --> 00:15:00.964
So in the world,
over 4 million of the 130 babies that

00:15:00.964 --> 00:15:04.753
are born every year die
in the first 28 days.

00:15:04.753 --> 00:15:10.380
And one of the primary causes today is
low birth weight, premature babies.

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:14.905
Premature babies do not have enough
subcutaneous fat in their body to

00:15:14.905 --> 00:15:16.940
regulate their temperature.

00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:22.071
So the normative technique for
the last over 100 years has been

00:15:22.071 --> 00:15:28.980
to put them in incubators where their body
temperature can be artificially regulated.

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:34.854
And so, incidentally, the incubator term
comes from the poultry industry where

00:15:34.854 --> 00:15:38.620
the technology was stolen from,
incubating eggs.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:43.263
And the modern day incubator
costs upwards of $20,000.

00:15:43.263 --> 00:15:47.792
But a lot of these babies are born in
developing countries with lots of social

00:15:47.792 --> 00:15:51.636
and economic issues, and
they cannot afford $20,000.

00:15:51.636 --> 00:15:56.013
So this team was asked to chop
two zeroes from the cost,

00:15:56.013 --> 00:15:59.960
make it into $200 instead of $20,000
in the theory that if you could

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:04.410
make it cheaper, a whole lot of these
countries that have the problem of

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:09.190
large scale infant mortality because of
premature birth, they could deal with it.

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:11.630
Now let's look at the journey that these

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:15.440
as students performed with
the design thinking guidelines.

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:20.660
So first and foremost,
instead of taking a typical approach of

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:25.360
a slashing cost by looking at the cost
of goods and making things cheaper, and

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:28.810
knock down, and
making the feature simpler.

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:32.890
They actually took the trouble to go
to the places where this was happening.

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:35.480
They went to rural India and
Bangladesh and

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:40.390
looked at these small regional
hospitals in fairly remote areas.

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:45.610
And much to their surprise, they found
that these hospitals had incubators,

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:46.980
they actually possess incubators.

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:51.290
But they also found something very
interesting that you must be noticing.

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:52.150
That there were no babies in them.

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:58.480
So that really puzzled them and they said
why are there no babies in the incubator?

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:03.944
And so they started digging into it and
they realized that actually the problem

00:17:03.944 --> 00:17:08.180
was that the people to the families to

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:13.040
whom the babies are born lived
in very tiny remote areas.

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:15.580
And they were day laborers and they could

00:17:16.620 --> 00:17:21.250
not afford the cost to bring
the baby to the regional hospitals.

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:25.900
So this is the point at which one of
the most important pivots took place.

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:30.220
They went against the brief that
their partners had given them and

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:33.780
they said,
we do not need a $200 baby incubator.

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:39.835
We need a $20 baby warmer that
can work without electricity.

00:17:39.835 --> 00:17:44.670
Particularly because they also notice
that a lot of these remote areas had

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:46.650
intermittent power.

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:49.740
So if you had something
that ran on electricity and

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:51.240
it failed, then the baby would die.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:54.830
So they reframed what
the problem brief was.

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:59.630
Then they came back and according to
the design principles they brainstormed.

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:02.618
The bottom left shows you that they
came up with many, many ideas.

00:18:02.618 --> 00:18:07.810
And they quickly went into
a rapid prototyping phase

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:13.250
where they tried out many, many
solutions in very rough and crude ways.

00:18:13.250 --> 00:18:17.740
And they bought materials from
grocery stores from other stores and

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:20.480
couple things together with Tupperware and

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:23.250
so on in order to explore the idea and
quickly test them.

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:26.780
And one of the ideas that came about was

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:31.600
why not make something like
a sleeping bag for a baby, but

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:36.500
also put an insert in it which is made
out of some kind of phase change material

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:42.500
which the first prototype is made out of
margarine and racks from a grocery store.

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:47.490
And if you put- Recrystallization
is something that can

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:51.491
be used to keep the baby warm, and hot
water is something you can find anywhere.

00:18:51.491 --> 00:18:56.232
And so that was the idea that won
out among a lot of contesting ideas.

00:18:56.232 --> 00:19:01.229
And just to give you a sense, they came
up with over 180 ideas, and they made

00:19:01.229 --> 00:19:06.253
over 80 prototypes in a matter of a few
weeks, so that's how first it moved.

00:19:06.253 --> 00:19:11.726
And then they didn't stop there,
they took this to the villages back and

00:19:11.726 --> 00:19:16.300
they learned a lot of lessons,
and lots of failure modes that

00:19:16.300 --> 00:19:21.056
would essentially take them down
in the last mile of concerns by

00:19:21.056 --> 00:19:26.090
many stakeholders who
constituted the stakeholder set.

00:19:26.090 --> 00:19:30.604
And they learned the definition
of the customer in that there's

00:19:30.604 --> 00:19:33.885
an entire set of people who can aid or
impede your cause.

00:19:33.885 --> 00:19:36.835
So they found out
the opinions of the midwives.

00:19:36.835 --> 00:19:38.415
They found out about the regional doctors.

00:19:38.415 --> 00:19:41.555
They found out about people who
were the regulators, etc, etc.

00:19:41.555 --> 00:19:43.505
They found out that people
are not lived with them,

00:19:43.505 --> 00:19:46.925
they might not understand
when the thing is too hard.

00:19:46.925 --> 00:19:53.090
So that changed their brief, so they came
back and kept prototyping fast forward.

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:58.190
These people, the year after the class
they just started a tiny little startup.

00:19:58.190 --> 00:20:06.270
They got a fund from a grant from
a nonprofit or a foundation.

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:09.390
And they came up with
a product that is now,

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:15.250
it's won every design there is to win.

00:20:15.250 --> 00:20:19.910
It's being distributed by big
players such as GE Healthcare.

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:22.862
It has saved tens of thousands of lives,

00:20:22.862 --> 00:20:25.260
if not hundreds of
thousands are backordered.

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:31.500
It's made a big different in
the issue of infant mortality.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:35.370
Now if you look at the system,

00:20:35.370 --> 00:20:38.760
if you're a hardcore engineer you wouldn't
be impressed it's very, very simple.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:45.550
Now if it is this simple, the question
to ask is then why did it elude

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:49.920
the medical community for
a 150 years, right?

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:53.730
It's not for want of billions of
dollars worth of research funding.

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:58.430
So here is the secret sauce.

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:01.940
The process involved actually
taking the trouble to go out and

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:06.950
seek insights without some,
and deep human considerations.

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:11.116
Without preconceived ideas,
without preconceived theories.

00:21:11.116 --> 00:21:16.319
Being naive and
open to what might really be happening.

00:21:16.319 --> 00:21:20.478
Reframing the problem statement
in terms of those and

00:21:20.478 --> 00:21:24.824
then being extremely generative
to create concepts and

00:21:24.824 --> 00:21:30.291
prototypes and then testing them so
you can generate insights again.

00:21:30.291 --> 00:21:36.623
But the mean, the big differentiator is
that they did not ask the wrong question.

00:21:36.623 --> 00:21:41.130
They did not say,
how can we design a cheap incubator?

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:44.840
They said, how can we keep babies alive?

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:46.858
And just that difference sends
you in a different path.

00:21:46.858 --> 00:21:51.904
So that in a nutshell is how design
thinking operates the long process

00:21:51.904 --> 00:21:58.353
with lots of tools, but at enough level of
abstraction, that's how it works, right?

00:21:58.353 --> 00:22:01.329
Now, there are many
types of challenges and

00:22:01.329 --> 00:22:05.609
one can ask, whether is design
thinking sufficient, right?

00:22:05.609 --> 00:22:10.760
Now if you think a little deeply,
one of the things that this

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:14.280
did is it looked at some of
the failure modes at the human level.

00:22:15.430 --> 00:22:18.760
And it looked at the lives of the people,
etc.

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:23.950
But one of the failure modes to
think about is that even though this

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:28.340
had a certain systems
thinking involved in it,

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:31.280
there is such a thing
as being system-blind.

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:33.870
Where your theory of success
doesn't take into account

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:38.650
the larger system dynamics and the complex
interactions of different factors.

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:43.190
So design thinking is absolutely beautiful

00:22:43.190 --> 00:22:48.225
when it comes to the interferes
between the system and the human.

00:22:48.225 --> 00:22:52.955
And often it can change certain
aspects about the system, but

00:22:52.955 --> 00:22:56.405
it's not necessarily a good tool to
change the larger system itself.

00:22:56.405 --> 00:23:03.240
For that, you need a tool
such as system acupuncture.

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:06.740
And in order to explain what that is,
I'm going to explain what

00:23:06.740 --> 00:23:11.350
some of the distinction are.

00:23:11.350 --> 00:23:14.890
This is the Cynefin Model by Snowden, and

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:17.895
it frames four different
types of challenges.

00:23:17.895 --> 00:23:20.135
One of them is simple,
the other's complicated,

00:23:20.135 --> 00:23:22.895
the third is complex, and
the fourth is chaotic.

00:23:22.895 --> 00:23:28.727
The simple is a self-evident system,
such as door lock, or French press, or.

00:23:28.727 --> 00:23:34.103
There if you don't need instructions

00:23:34.103 --> 00:23:37.971
in how to work that system.

00:23:37.971 --> 00:23:43.691
A complicated system is like a car or
Swiss watch, it's very complicated.

00:23:43.691 --> 00:23:47.170
Many parts, many, many coupled systems and

00:23:47.170 --> 00:23:50.567
you need expertise in
order to deal with it.

00:23:50.567 --> 00:23:55.374
But the subsystems are predictable in what

00:23:55.374 --> 00:24:00.173
they do and you can bring
a whole lot of what is called,

00:24:00.173 --> 00:24:03.925
deterministic thinking, in solving these.

00:24:03.925 --> 00:24:06.074
Complex problems however,

00:24:06.074 --> 00:24:11.980
are ones where there's a dance
between many different components.

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:16.640
And unless you understand the dynamics
of that dance, the chances

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:21.510
that you would be able to influence
it in the way you want to is low.

00:24:21.510 --> 00:24:25.663
And you need to understand it
in terms of the relationships.

00:24:25.663 --> 00:24:29.788
So it's like a forest where many,
many components have symbiotic

00:24:29.788 --> 00:24:34.516
relationships and that gives you
the larger construct which is the forest.

00:24:34.516 --> 00:24:37.024
And chaotic is when something is happening

00:24:37.024 --> 00:24:41.214
because of which the relationships
are changing very, very quickly.

00:24:41.214 --> 00:24:46.133
Like a forest, fire where the processes
are something that are very becoming

00:24:46.133 --> 00:24:49.994
increasingly unconstrained and
things are changing very,

00:24:49.994 --> 00:24:55.140
very quickly where the influence
you have on the system is very low.

00:24:55.140 --> 00:25:01.000
Now, it turns out that most of our systems
happen to befall in the complex realm, but

00:25:02.080 --> 00:25:08.590
most of our decision making cultures,
and tools, are for complicated systems.

00:25:08.590 --> 00:25:13.080
So we bring the tools of one type of
system to a different type of problem,

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:18.050
and there lies one of the reasons
why we are unable to come

00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:20.200
up with systemic solutions
to systemic problems.

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:25.960
And if you have a system that
is going into chaotic states,

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:28.110
you need arrest it very, very quickly.

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:33.730
And because the level of influence
that you have, it drops very rapidly.

00:25:33.730 --> 00:25:37.890
So imagine you're cooking and
suddenly the kitchen catches fire,

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:41.360
that is not the time to go do your dishes.

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:44.915
That's the time to put out the fire
before, and you have a small window of

00:25:44.915 --> 00:25:50.040
time, turns out that a lot of our
larger systems are entering already

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:55.235
into chaotic state so we need to find
interventions very, very quickly to those.

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:03.150
And I would argue that there's
complexity in the simplest of things.

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:07.335
If you are at a dining table
conversation with an extended family,

00:26:07.335 --> 00:26:10.335
that's a complex system onto itself,
right?

00:26:10.335 --> 00:26:14.615
But a lot of the big things that we
talked about in setting the stage,

00:26:14.615 --> 00:26:19.122
we as organizations, as in-markets
that are changing that rapidly,

00:26:19.122 --> 00:26:21.695
we are definitely in the complex realm.

00:26:21.695 --> 00:26:26.041
So, the mindset to have is watch out for

00:26:26.041 --> 00:26:31.855
whether a system is complex or
simply complicated.

00:26:31.855 --> 00:26:34.398
And if they are systemic challenges,

00:26:34.398 --> 00:26:39.324
they ought to be met with systemic
solutions, and if they involve scale,

00:26:39.324 --> 00:26:43.310
then you have to have your
solutions involve scale as well.

00:26:43.310 --> 00:26:45.705
So if you look at this another way,

00:26:45.705 --> 00:26:50.589
we can see that there are many ways
in which to categorize challenges.

00:26:50.589 --> 00:26:53.157
But there are challenges
that are bounded in nature,

00:26:53.157 --> 00:26:56.790
they are very predictable systems,
however complicated.

00:26:56.790 --> 00:27:00.180
And the business as usual systems
work pretty well out there.

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:06.440
When you have ill-defined challenges but
where the boundary conditions

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:12.860
are fairly tight, such as how do you
create a service that is engaging

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:18.160
to a diverse customer set,
now that's a pretty open-ended question.

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:20.780
However, it's somewhat bounded
because all you're dealing with is

00:27:20.780 --> 00:27:23.190
engagement of a certain customer set.

00:27:23.190 --> 00:27:26.280
Then, that is something
that innovation tools,

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:28.540
such as design thinking is sufficient.

00:27:28.540 --> 00:27:33.470
However, if you're trying to transform the
system itself, that's a class C kind of

00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:39.140
challenge and that's where you
need more systems thinking and

00:27:39.140 --> 00:27:43.550
more tools that deal with
the complexity and scale.

00:27:43.550 --> 00:27:47.790
Incidentally, for those of you who
are in organization types that

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:52.060
have to deal with business success,
it's also closely tied

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:55.620
to the difference between the Red Ocean
Strategies and Blue Ocean Strategies.

00:27:55.620 --> 00:28:01.250
Red Ocean Strategies relates to strategies
where it's where the margins are low,

00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:04.300
there's very little differentiation
whether what is red

00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:07.310
with the blood of sharks
going out at the same food.

00:28:07.310 --> 00:28:10.110
A Blue Ocean Strategy is where
you're able to innovate and

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:14.190
create a space where there
are relatively few competitors and

00:28:14.190 --> 00:28:18.080
you're creating value rather
than simply capturing value.

00:28:18.080 --> 00:28:21.540
And you're creating markets that
you get to write the rule books in.

00:28:21.540 --> 00:28:25.730
The competitors will come, but
if you create an innovation capacity,

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:28.700
you have created a capacity
to stay one step ahead.

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:31.060
So these two concepts are actually tied,

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:36.350
which also implies that business
success is also closely related

00:28:36.350 --> 00:28:40.990
to working towards future systems that
are desirable and there are ways to do it.

00:28:40.990 --> 00:28:45.250
Now, we have that at
Stanford Change Labs which I head.

00:28:45.250 --> 00:28:49.240
We've come up with a methodology which
we call System Acupuncture which has

00:28:49.240 --> 00:28:52.990
many tools and a process architecture
that I'm going to a little bit.

00:28:52.990 --> 00:28:56.230
But it involves a theory,
a framework, a set of processes,

00:28:56.230 --> 00:28:59.640
and tools that integrate systems thinking,
design thinking, and

00:28:59.640 --> 00:29:04.460
an architecture around scale,
a very nuanced way of looking at scale.

00:29:05.690 --> 00:29:09.770
Building in a lot of theories from other
fields, such as resilience theory,

00:29:09.770 --> 00:29:11.770
leverage points, economic complexity,

00:29:11.770 --> 00:29:15.070
but making it very accessible in
ways in which one could use it.

00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:20.520
But also recognizing that
the behavioral component and

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:23.700
the social component is a very,
very large component and

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:28.500
whether or not an innovation diffuses
through a community is very important.

00:29:28.500 --> 00:29:31.090
And there's a ton of research out there,
risk and

00:29:31.090 --> 00:29:33.720
return into principles and design tools.

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:35.220
So we do that as well.

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:39.430
And we are reframing what technology
business and organizational

00:29:39.430 --> 00:29:44.440
change strategy looks like by looking
at the non-linear IT [INAUDIBLE] and

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:46.250
how do you change cultures.

00:29:46.250 --> 00:29:48.660
But also using non-linear technology,

00:29:48.660 --> 00:29:51.900
new business models such
as platform thinking, etc.

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:58.160
And a certain kind of integration into,
not just looking at single outcomes, but

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:03.810
simultaneously looking at multi
outcome strategies in elegant ways.

00:30:03.810 --> 00:30:06.340
So that is what the process looks like.

00:30:06.340 --> 00:30:11.960
But what we are, somewhat, fetishistic
about is are we creating a skill impact?

00:30:13.070 --> 00:30:18.760
We are recreating a deep
transformation in the system behavior,

00:30:18.760 --> 00:30:23.180
and if the current system is headed
towards a certain predictable future that

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:28.620
we do not like, or we are concerned about
the stability of the resilience of,

00:30:28.620 --> 00:30:34.360
can we do something to send it
towards a different future and

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:38.190
create a position for
ourselves where it's win-win.

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:44.750
We're able to deal with impending
changes or potential changes in ways

00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:50.100
that they can navigate those
waters with better seamanship.

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:55.118
And can we actually take charge of
the future and position ourselves,

00:30:55.118 --> 00:31:00.064
so that we have more influence in
which way the future actually goes?

00:31:00.064 --> 00:31:06.479
So the direction makes a big difference,
but also can we come up with outcomes?

00:31:06.479 --> 00:31:09.138
The delta is a science of change.

00:31:09.138 --> 00:31:12.098
So if we were to take
on aspirational goals,

00:31:12.098 --> 00:31:16.898
and part of innovation is creating
extremely aspirational goals, and

00:31:16.898 --> 00:31:21.538
then working really hard to meet them and
my argument is that actually

00:31:21.538 --> 00:31:26.620
having greater aspirations is
a very big component of innovation.

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:27.960
If you did not shoot for

00:31:27.960 --> 00:31:31.960
the moon, it would be unlikely that you'd
get to the top of even a tall building.

00:31:31.960 --> 00:31:36.680
You'd need a large target in order to
get there, to direct your thinking.

00:31:36.680 --> 00:31:40.280
So if you have a large
chasm between the current

00:31:42.050 --> 00:31:46.280
massive challenge in the future,
how do we get to that?

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:47.600
How do we bridge that chasm?

00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:52.841
And of course,
you cannot jump a chasm in two leaps.

00:31:52.841 --> 00:31:54.103
A single leap.

00:31:54.103 --> 00:31:58.470
You have to cross a chasm
in a single leap.

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:01.147
So all you need to build a bridge.

00:32:01.147 --> 00:32:05.951
So let's see what you need to do in terms
of this philosophy of system acupuncture.

00:32:05.951 --> 00:32:10.663
First of all, the term comes with
the admission that brute force methods

00:32:10.663 --> 00:32:15.025
are not going to work because you
simply do not have the resources.

00:32:15.025 --> 00:32:20.291
You have to be elegant in
how you use resources.

00:32:20.291 --> 00:32:25.460
And so the metaphor is acupuncture
where you look into the system

00:32:25.460 --> 00:32:29.510
with enough intelligence to have a theory
about where you should place your

00:32:29.510 --> 00:32:34.140
interventions in clever ways such that
you change the behavior of the system.

00:32:34.140 --> 00:32:38.074
And you get the outcomes in
the most resource effective and

00:32:38.074 --> 00:32:41.993
actionable ways,
which is also very, very leveraged.

00:32:41.993 --> 00:32:43.590
So if you look at this chasm,

00:32:43.590 --> 00:32:49.170
the first step to look at is you need
to envision a future that is different.

00:32:49.170 --> 00:32:53.150
Because you are doing that
implicitly anyways even if,

00:32:53.150 --> 00:32:56.250
by placing your belief in businesses,
usual because

00:32:56.250 --> 00:33:01.130
you have bought into the vision that
the business is usual implicitly has.

00:33:01.130 --> 00:33:05.168
But if you're trying to change the future,
it is worth taking the time and

00:33:05.168 --> 00:33:08.055
using the tools in order
to cast the future anyways.

00:33:08.055 --> 00:33:11.035
And then you're building a bridge
that involves a few steps.

00:33:11.035 --> 00:33:13.165
So you envision the future.

00:33:13.165 --> 00:33:19.549
So there's another step before that,
is creating the preconditions for success.

00:33:19.549 --> 00:33:24.349
But then the steps in the innovation
process is envisioning the future

00:33:24.349 --> 00:33:30.170
from a systems prospective,
understanding what makes the system tick.

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:34.740
And then doing the work it takes to
create a theory of change in terms of

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:36.760
systems acupuncture points.

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:40.290
Now that you have the makings
of a strategy at a high level,

00:33:40.290 --> 00:33:44.040
saying that if we change this side and
that, theoretically we should change.

00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:45.410
Then you design your intervention and

00:33:45.410 --> 00:33:49.911
that turns into more higher level
fidelities of platform architectures, or

00:33:49.911 --> 00:33:55.730
process architectures, or organization
change theories of processes and so on.

00:33:55.730 --> 00:33:59.050
It can go in many different directions,
data plans,

00:33:59.050 --> 00:34:02.250
market models, or what have you,
product services.

00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:08.470
But then you have to turn it into action,
that is, that has the potential to scale.

00:34:08.470 --> 00:34:13.690
And you have to think about scale it every
step, and essentially bake and scale.

00:34:13.690 --> 00:34:17.680
So that's something that we're
going to talk about as well.

00:34:17.680 --> 00:34:20.930
So that essentially is the arc.

00:34:20.930 --> 00:34:25.180
But when you do that,
what we do is we take a certain lens.

00:34:25.180 --> 00:34:29.020
We are looking at the behavior at
the system level and the human level.

00:34:29.020 --> 00:34:31.938
We look at everything
through the scale lens.

00:34:31.938 --> 00:34:36.878
If we look at the ecosystem of what's
happening in the larger ecosystem

00:34:36.878 --> 00:34:41.007
not just put the blinders on
our technology, or our focus.

00:34:41.007 --> 00:34:45.454
And we definitely take the innovation lens
of what can be changed in a radical way,

00:34:45.454 --> 00:34:47.007
in a disproportionate way.

00:34:47.007 --> 00:34:49.642
We do that in the time we
are understanding the system,

00:34:49.642 --> 00:34:53.273
we do that when we're strategizing and
when we're designing solutions.

00:34:53.273 --> 00:34:55.624
So those are the lens we use.

00:34:55.624 --> 00:35:02.130
And so let me say that you could do all of
this and still have another failure mode.

00:35:02.130 --> 00:35:08.520
You could be scale-blind and
that means that you could

00:35:08.520 --> 00:35:14.080
do something where you somehow had a view

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:18.440
that it's going to scale automatically,
just because the problem is scaled.

00:35:18.440 --> 00:35:19.730
And that's what motivates you.

00:35:19.730 --> 00:35:21.780
Your solution is going to scale as well.

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:24.004
And you're actually going to
be able to move the needle.

00:35:24.004 --> 00:35:28.765
Well, what we understand from our
research is scale is inordinately hard

00:35:28.765 --> 00:35:30.548
to stuff in after the fact.

00:35:30.548 --> 00:35:34.307
The DNA of your solution is
if it doesn't have scale,

00:35:34.307 --> 00:35:38.960
then you have to get inordinately
lucky to have scale.

00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:44.505
So if you are not banking on blind luck,

00:35:44.505 --> 00:35:49.505
then you need to incorporate
from the principle of scale.

00:35:49.505 --> 00:35:53.105
And first and foremost,
you need to become scale-minded.

00:35:53.105 --> 00:35:55.720
You have to build in
scalability at the onset.

00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:57.730
You need to obsess about
it at every scale and

00:35:57.730 --> 00:36:02.820
you need to build in non-linearity if
your problems involve non-linearity.

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:07.020
Now there are many aspects to scale,
many dimensions to scale.

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:09.754
One can't just go by
traditional metrics of numbers.

00:36:09.754 --> 00:36:13.670
You have to first ask yourself,
can you enter it?

00:36:13.670 --> 00:36:19.170
Where you have no option of entering
at scale, what is going to allow you to

00:36:19.170 --> 00:36:23.450
do scaling, that distinction itself
is a very important distinction.

00:36:23.450 --> 00:36:26.170
But scale is always tied to time.

00:36:26.170 --> 00:36:27.320
It's inseparable from time.

00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:30.210
Because if you had unlimited resources and
unlimited time,

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:32.510
you would eventually get scale.

00:36:32.510 --> 00:36:36.470
But we don't have 100 years and
we don't have unlimited amounts of time.

00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:42.490
So there's a time constant built in into
scale, so we can't be time-blind either.

00:36:42.490 --> 00:36:45.610
Particularly, if the problems
are running away from us or

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:47.060
the industry's changing rapidly.

00:36:47.060 --> 00:36:49.789
We need to understand
the nuance of time as well.

00:36:50.800 --> 00:36:56.660
So if we have to talk about some of these
things as mindsets for the transformative

00:36:56.660 --> 00:36:59.880
leader, at least some of the top there
are many, many different things.

00:36:59.880 --> 00:37:05.127
But some of the things to worry about is,
are we being impact minded?

00:37:05.127 --> 00:37:07.599
Are we really being change minded?

00:37:07.599 --> 00:37:10.782
Are we wearing the innovation hat?

00:37:10.782 --> 00:37:15.975
Or are we being risk averse to the point

00:37:15.975 --> 00:37:20.780
of being risky, where we have baked in so

00:37:20.780 --> 00:37:25.250
many business processes that abhor
failure or are scared of risk.

00:37:25.250 --> 00:37:30.830
That we've also brought down the ceiling
for what kind of ideas can be brought.

00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:35.060
And either we are losing
out an opportunity for

00:37:35.060 --> 00:37:39.770
occupying a completely different
position or leaving ourselves wide open

00:37:39.770 --> 00:37:43.930
to other people who would be
innovative and essentially upstage us.

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:46.510
We have to be system minded.

00:37:46.510 --> 00:37:52.518
And that's a whole perceptual lens by
which one can look at the world and

00:37:52.518 --> 00:37:53.429
our work.

00:37:53.429 --> 00:37:58.215
And this is a very different way
of life than procedural ways

00:37:58.215 --> 00:38:01.100
that look at complicated systems.

00:38:01.100 --> 00:38:06.792
And for those of you in the technology
field, a lot of our training trains us for

00:38:06.792 --> 00:38:11.040
complicated systems but not for
kind of complex systems.

00:38:11.040 --> 00:38:15.280
We need to be scale minded,
time minded, and process minded.

00:38:15.280 --> 00:38:19.295
Because if we have a good sense of process
and we hack the process itself, and

00:38:19.295 --> 00:38:21.540
we're nuanced about how we use a process,

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:24.350
process can be a great proxy for
leadership.

00:38:24.350 --> 00:38:28.590
It gets you those things that
extraordinary leadership gets you.

00:38:28.590 --> 00:38:33.240
And because of our challenges,
this is not a single spot.

00:38:33.240 --> 00:38:37.320
It's not a single stakeholder spot,
so we need to find ways in way to be

00:38:37.320 --> 00:38:40.770
very co-creative and
engaging multiple stakeholders.

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:44.340
We need to envision alternate futures and
drive towards them.

00:38:44.340 --> 00:38:48.390
And there is a huge part to
create being facilitating and

00:38:48.390 --> 00:38:52.480
catalyzing action, to the extent that
you're also creating leadership modalities

00:38:52.480 --> 00:38:57.720
among people who might be very young or
might not have very much experience.

00:38:57.720 --> 00:39:01.340
But you need to have them fall
into leadership modalities.

00:39:01.340 --> 00:39:05.120
And ultimately, you're creating
an ecosystem for systemic change.

00:39:06.350 --> 00:39:11.450
So what we're looking at is
essentially that which a certain

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:16.080
kind of leadership that will be
able to increase the innovation and

00:39:16.080 --> 00:39:20.120
the transformation capacity of your team.

00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:24.910
First of all, of yourself,
then the team, then your division.

00:39:24.910 --> 00:39:29.857
Then your organization, including what
position your organization has in

00:39:29.857 --> 00:39:34.495
the industry, the entire industry itself,
and in multi-industry,

00:39:34.495 --> 00:39:38.020
multi-stakeholder, multi-sector
ecosystems.

00:39:38.020 --> 00:39:42.386
And all of that, the degree to
which we can be innovated on and

00:39:42.386 --> 00:39:45.304
transformed is up to how we approach it.

00:39:45.304 --> 00:39:48.018
And what kind of methodological
tools we bring to it and

00:39:48.018 --> 00:39:50.083
what kind of value systems we bring to it.

00:39:50.083 --> 00:39:52.450
So ultimately what we are trying to do

00:39:52.450 --> 00:39:56.689
Is move the needle on extremely
profound challenges and

00:39:56.689 --> 00:40:01.739
create a world where we have a place
in doing excellent business and

00:40:01.739 --> 00:40:07.443
we have a position which is resilient and
point it towards better futures.

00:40:07.443 --> 00:40:10.306
So we need to worry about
potency of intervention.

00:40:10.306 --> 00:40:15.240
Whether the scale is
relevant in times that

00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:20.046
are relevant and with a diffusion scale.

00:40:20.046 --> 00:40:25.449
And the degree to which is integrated in
the existing complex systems rather than

00:40:25.449 --> 00:40:30.863
of being a figment of anti-imagination
which is very, very hard to actualize.

00:40:30.863 --> 00:40:33.936
So I'll end there and
