00:40:36.074 --> 00:40:41.890
And so I'm looking at the questions,
and there are some recurring questions.

00:40:41.890 --> 00:40:46.000
There are people who are asking about
examples of an exponential problem.

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:50.350
Well, an exponential problem is
essentially where, when you look at it

00:40:50.350 --> 00:40:55.200
from a systems perspective,
it is typically caused because of some

00:40:55.200 --> 00:41:00.370
kind of a feedback loop where a causes b,

00:41:00.370 --> 00:41:04.560
b causes c and
maybe there are many steps later.

00:41:04.560 --> 00:41:08.531
But if a causes b and b causes c,
but c causes a again,

00:41:08.531 --> 00:41:12.432
then the effect is also
contributing to the cause.

00:41:12.432 --> 00:41:17.024
And it creates a feedback loop and
particularly if you have a high gain rate,

00:41:17.024 --> 00:41:19.843
you can actually get
an exponential problem.

00:41:19.843 --> 00:41:25.055
So in the, and at a planetary scale,
the fact that we have lost

00:41:25.055 --> 00:41:30.371
a whole lot of our polar ice caps
means that a whole lot of white,

00:41:30.371 --> 00:41:36.291
reflective landmass is being replaced
by dark absorptive landmass,

00:41:36.291 --> 00:41:39.510
and the heat gain is increasing.

00:41:39.510 --> 00:41:42.970
Which means that the temperature of
the oceans is increasing at a much faster

00:41:44.100 --> 00:41:47.470
rate and
causing the ice to melt even faster.

00:41:47.470 --> 00:41:49.239
So this is an example of a feedback loop.

00:41:49.239 --> 00:41:52.679
But if you find any
rapidly changing equation,

00:41:52.679 --> 00:41:56.384
particularly if it's tied
to a population issue.

00:41:56.384 --> 00:41:59.469
So, if you have a finite
resource such as water or

00:41:59.469 --> 00:42:02.254
energy, energy is not exactly finite, but

00:42:02.254 --> 00:42:06.941
at any given time the capacity over
a short period of time might be finite.

00:42:06.941 --> 00:42:10.761
But then in that time frame,
if you have a rapid increase because of

00:42:10.761 --> 00:42:13.845
population pressures or
an influx into the city etc.

00:42:13.845 --> 00:42:18.533
Then you're seeing a certain kind of,
you might have feedback

00:42:18.533 --> 00:42:23.308
loops where some issue is being
raised not at a linear scale, but

00:42:23.308 --> 00:42:27.980
either at an exponential scale or
with step functions.

00:42:27.980 --> 00:42:30.580
And you see that both in positive and
negative ways.

00:42:30.580 --> 00:42:36.260
You could get sudden collapses in
systems such as if you get a functions

00:42:36.260 --> 00:42:41.160
in the level of density in a city,
the entire transportation system can

00:42:41.160 --> 00:42:45.430
come to a grinding halt and
that's a non-linear effect as well.

00:42:45.430 --> 00:42:47.840
